.TH OTPME-CONTROLD 8 "2026-11-15" "OTPme 0.3.0a289" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-controld \- start, stop and watch the OTPme daemons
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-controld
[\fIoptions\fR]
.B start
.br
.B otpme-controld
[\fIoptions\fR]
.B stop
[\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIseconds\fR] [\fB\-k\fR]
.br
.B otpme-controld
[\fIoptions\fR]
.BR restart " | " reload
[\fIdaemon\fR]
.br
.B otpme-controld
.B status
.SH DESCRIPTION
.na
.nh
.B otpme\-controld
runs the OTPme daemons of this node or host.
It runs as root.
It starts the other daemons as the OTPme user, except
.BR confd ,
which keeps root.
See \fBARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW\fR in
.BR otpme (7)
for what each daemon does.
.PP
On a node it starts, in this order:
.BR hostd ", " mgmtd ", " joind ", " scriptd ", " syncd ", " authd ", " ssod ", " clusterd ", " httpd ", " fsd ", " backupd " and " confd .
.B clusterd
starts
.BR ldapd ", " idled " and " racd
when the cluster is ready, and controld watches them from then on.
On a host it starts
.B hostd
and
.BR confd ,
plus
.B httpd
with \fBSSO_SERVER\fR and
.B backupd
with \fBBACKUP_SERVER\fR, see
.BR otpme.conf (5).
.PP
Before the daemons it starts the index database and the cache server, see \fBAUTOSTART_INDEX\fR and \fBAUTOSTART_CACHE\fR in
.BR otpme.conf (5).
On the master node it also holds the floating IP of the site, and starts FreeRADIUS and haproxy where they are enabled.
.PP
controld checks its daemons every \fBCONTROLD_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL\fR seconds.
It starts a daemon that is not running, and restarts one that does not answer, see \fBDAEMON\fR in
.BR otpme.conf (5).
.PP
Only root can use it.
The host has to be a member of a realm, see
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
.hy
.ad
.SH COMMANDS
.na
.nh
.TP
.B start
Start controld and its daemons, in the background.
It says \(dqAlready running.\(dq if controld runs already.
The command returns before the daemons are up; the log shows when they are.
.br
With \fB\-dD\fR controld stays in the foreground, see \fBOPTIONS\fR.
.TP
\fBstop\fR [\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIseconds\fR] [\fB\-k\fR]
Stop controld.
It stops its daemons in the reverse order, removes the floating IP, and stops the cache and the index database it started.
.br
A daemon gets 30 seconds to quit, then it is killed.
The command waits \fB\-\-timeout\fR seconds for all of this (default: \fB60\fR) and fails if controld still runs then.
controld goes on stopping.
.br
With \fB\-k\fR the command waits half the timeout and then kills controld and all its daemons.
.TP
.B restart \fR[\fIdaemon\fR]
Without a daemon: stop and start controld and all daemons.
If controld still runs after 70 seconds, it is killed.
.br
With a daemon: controld stops that daemon and starts it again.
A daemon reads most settings when it starts, for example \fBauthd_workers\fR, see \fBDaemon Tuning\fR in
.BR otpme (7).
.B clusterd
is not restarted on the master node; the log says so.
.TP
.B reload \fR[\fIdaemon\fR]
Without a daemon: controld reads
.BR otpme.conf (5)
again and tells every daemon to do the same.
This is the same as sending SIGHUP to controld.
A daemon that is new on the list, for example httpd after \fBSSO_SERVER\fR was set on a host, is started at the next heartbeat.
One that is no longer on the list is stopped.
.br
With a daemon: only that daemon reloads.
.br
A daemon that cannot reload is restarted.
So is one that does not answer within 15 seconds.
.PP
With a daemon, \fBrestart\fR and \fBreload\fR fail if controld does not run or if there is no daemon of that name.
.TP
.B status
Print \(dqRunning.\(dq or \(dqStopped.\(dq.
It says nothing about the single daemons.
The exit code is 0 while controld runs and 1 when it is stopped, so a script can ask.
.hy
.ad
.SH OPTIONS
.na
.nh
The options go before the command.
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIseconds\fR
For \fBstop\fR: how long to wait (default: \fB60\fR).
.TP
.B \-k
For \fBstop\fR: kill what has not quit after half the timeout.
.TP
\fB\-l\fR \fIfile\fR
Write the log of controld and of all daemons to this file.
Without it every daemon writes its own file in \fBLOG_DIR\fR, or the log goes to a syslog server, see \fBSYSLOG\fR in
.BR otpme.conf (5).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-index\-start
Do not start and stop the index database.
It has to run already, or controld does not start.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-cache\-start
Do not start and stop the cache server.
It has to run already, or controld does not start.
.TP
.B \-\-cache\-flush
Empty the cache when controld starts.
This is the default, see \fBFLUSH_CACHE_ON_START\fR in
.BR otpme.conf (5).
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-changed\-objects
hostd syncs objects even if they change while it syncs.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-floating\-ip
On the master node: do not remove the floating IP when controld stops.
.TP
.B \-dD
Stay in the foreground.
Add \fB\-dd\fR to see the log on the terminal, and more \fB\-d\fR options to see more.
Ctrl+C stops controld and its daemons.
See
.BR otpme (1)
for all debug options, and for \fB\-\-color\-logs\fR, \fB\-\-log\-filter\fR and \fB\-\-debug\-daemons\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SH SIGNALS
.na
.nh
.TP
.B SIGTERM
Stop, as \fBstop\fR does.
.TP
.B SIGINT
The same.
This is Ctrl+C in the foreground.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload, as \fBreload\fR without a daemon does.
.PP
controld ignores all three while it starts its daemons.
.hy
.ad
.SH EXAMPLES
.na
.nh
.TP
.B otpme\-controld start
Start everything in the background.
.TP
.B otpme\-controld \-dD \-dd start
Start in the foreground and show the log.
.TP
.B otpme\-controld \-dD \-dd \-\-log\-filter authd,mgmtd start
The same, but show only the log of authd and mgmtd.
.TP
.B otpme\-controld reload httpd
Let httpd read its configuration again, for example after the SSO certificate changed.
.TP
.B otpme\-controld restart authd
Restart authd, for example after \fBauthd_workers\fR was changed.
.TP
.B otpme\-controld stop \-\-timeout 120 \-k
Stop, and kill what still runs after 60 seconds.
.hy
.ad
.SH FILES
.na
.nh
.TP
\fI/var/run/otpme/pidfiles/otpme\-controld.pid\fR
The PID of controld.
The directory is \fBRUN_DIR\fR, see
.BR otpme.conf (5).
.TP
\fI/var/log/otpme/otpme\-daemon.log\fR
The log of each daemon, without \fB\-l\fR and without \fB\-dD\fR.
The directory is \fBLOG_DIR\fR.
.TP
\fIdeploy/systemd/otpme.service\fR
A systemd unit that runs \fBotpme\-controld start\fR, \fBstop\fR and \fBreload\fR.
.hy
.ad
